<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Brazil</h1><p class="st-meta">1815 CE – 1822 CE · capital Rio de Janeiro</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The kingdom created when Brazil was raised to equal status with Portugal, with Rio de Janeiro as the capital of the whole Portuguese monarchy.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Portuguese royal court had moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a> in 1808 to escape <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>. On 16 December 1815 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-vi-of-portugal">Prince Regent John</a> raised <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/new-republic-brazil">Brazil</a> to a kingdom within the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/united-kingdom-of-portugal-brazil-and-the-algarves">United Kingdom of Portugal, Brazil and the Algarves</a>, ending colonial status for the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-brazil">State of Brazil</a>. He was acclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> VI in Rio in 1818.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio</a> grew into a royal capital with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bank-of-brazil">Bank of Brazil</a>, a royal library, a botanical garden and a French artistic mission. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil's</a> ports had been open to friendly nations since 1808. The crown crushed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-pernambuco">Republic of Pernambuco</a> in 1817 and annexed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/eastern-province">Banda Oriental</a> as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/cisplatina">Cisplatina</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">Slavery</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> continued to expand.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The liberal revolution in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porto">Porto</a> in 1820 forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-vi-of-portugal">John VI</a> to return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> in 1821, leaving his son Pedro as regent. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cortes">Portuguese Cortes</a> sought to reduce <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil's</a> status, Pedro declared independence on 7 September 1822 and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazilian-empire">Empire of Brazil</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-brazil.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Brazil" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: This vector image includes elements that have been taken or adapted from this file · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Kingdom_of_Brazil_(1822).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1815 CE – 1822 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Rio de Janeiro</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>John VI, Pedro I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Portuguese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/brazil">Brazil</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Reino do Brasil</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1818&amp;focus=kingdom-of-brazil" aria-label="Kingdom of Brazil on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3525 3023 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Brazil lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3845 3279L3944 2917M3845 3279L3944 2917M3845 3279L3845 3279M3845 3279L3846 3279"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3944 2917h0M3944 2917h0M3845 3279h0M3846 3279h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3845 3279h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3845 3279h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Rio de Janeiro. <a href="/atlas?year=1818&amp;focus=kingdom-of-brazil">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
